<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Motya</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 397 BCE (approx.) · capital Motya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Phoenician island city in the Stagnone lagoon of western Sicily, a major Punic trading base until Dionysius I of Syracuse destroyed it in 397 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Motya was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> in the late 8th century BCE on the small island of San Pantaleo, off the western tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Like other Phoenician sites, it chose an offshore island with sheltered anchorage close to a native hinterland, here the Elymian towns of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/segesta">Segesta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eryx">Eryx</a>. It soon came within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage's</a> sphere.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Motya was a busy port and craft centre, with dye works, potteries and a small artificial basin. A causeway linked it to the mainland, and walls with towers enclosed the island in the 6th century BCE. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tophet">tophet</a>, a sacred precinct for child burials, and its temples show Phoenician religion; the marble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motya-charioteer">Motya Charioteer</a>, a Greek masterpiece of about 470 BCE, was found there in 1979.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 397 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I of Syracuse</a> besieged Motya, building a mole across the lagoon and using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower">siege towers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a>; the city fell after fierce street fighting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> did not rebuild it and moved the survivors to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lilybaeum">Lilybaeum</a> on the mainland. The island was bought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-whitaker">Joseph Whitaker</a>, who began its excavation in the early 20th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/motya.jpg" alt="Motya" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Verity Cridland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Motya_(8).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 397 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Motya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Phoenician</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Motye, Mozia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-598&amp;focus=motya" aria-label="Motya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4991 816 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Motya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5311 1072L5259 1106M5311 1072L5383 1099M5311 1072L5310 1074"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0M5383 1099h0M5310 1074h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5311 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5311 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-598&amp;focus=motya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
